    "title": "A NEW CULTURE",
    "image": "https://i0.wp.com/www.anpigenova.it/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Annotazione-2020-03-04-171632.png?fit=422%2C297&ssl=1",
    "author": "ELIO VITTORINI (Translation of EDITOR)",
    "subtitle": "First editorial of the cultural magazine \"Il Politecnico\", a publication founded in Italy right after the Second World War.",
    "references": ["2"],
    "content": "(September 29, 1945). No longer a culture that consoles in suffering, but a culture that protects from suffering, that fights it and eliminates it.\n\nFor a while, it will be difficult to say whether anyone or anything has won in this war. But certainly, there is much that has lost, and it is evident how it has lost. The dead, if we count them, are more children than soldiers; the rubble is of cities that had twenty-five centuries of life; of houses and libraries, of monuments, of cathedrals, of all the forms through which the civil progress of man has passed; and the fields where most blood has been shed are called Mauthausen, Majdanek, Buchenwald, Dachau.\n\nWhose is the most serious defeat in all that has happened? There certainly was something that, through the centuries, had taught us to consider the existence of children sacred. It had also taught us that every civil achievement of man was sacred; the same for bread; the same for work. And if now millions of children have been killed, if so much that was sacred has nevertheless been struck and destroyed, the defeat is above all that of this \"thing\" that taught us their inviolability. Is it not above all that of this \"thing\" that taught us their inviolability?\n\nThis \"thing\", I want to say it immediately, is nothing other than culture: it which has been Greek thought, Hellenism, Romanism, Latin Christianity, medieval Christianity, humanism, the Reformation, the Enlightenment, liberalism, etc., and which today clusters around the names of Thomas Mann and Benedetto Croce, Benda, Huizinga, Dewey, Maritain, Bernanos and Unamuno, Lin Yutang and Santayana, Valéry, Gide and Berdyaev.\n\nThere is no crime committed by fascism that this culture had not already long since taught us to condemn. And if fascism has had the opportunity to commit all the crimes that this culture had already long since taught us to condemn, must we not ask this very culture how and why fascism was able to commit them?\n\