    "subtitle": "Art, productive forces and the urgency of a new Avant-Garde.",
    "references": ["1"],
    "content": "The incessant development of productive forces has once again highlighted the social character of art.\n\nProductive forces encompass both the means of production and labor power within the same category. The former refers to tools, machinery, factory organization, and labor technology in general; the latter includes not only human energy but also technical knowledge, skills, and accumulated experience.\n\nAnd what is the history of art but the history of technological development, the refinement of skill, and the consolidation of experience?\n\nIn every epoch of human history, productive forces have been unleashed by the establishment of new relations of production between existing social classes. Each region of the world has done so through its own institutions, laws, and customs, but always with its elevated classes positioned above others. Art, as a special property of productive development, has played a fundamental role in the rise, stagnation, and fall of these relations.\n\nHowever, the establishment and consolidation of capitalist productive relations marked a turning point in human history. Since then, the appropriation of the means of production has become increasingly private, while the act of producing has become increasingly social, as the economic regime gathers more and more workers into the same production chains. This same shift became evident in art with the rise of the avant-gardes, whose very nature was collective and coordinated artistic production. It is also clear in their legacy, as the same contradiction operating at the productive level also applies to the artistic one: originality is no longer possible under individuality, just as production is not.\n\nDuring the past century, alongside the historical possibility for human beings to reappropriate themselves—leaving behind an old order based on internal class divisions—there also appeared the potential for culture to once again become a truly integrating part of their exist... (line truncated to 2000 chars)
